<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<assembly manifestVersion="1.0" xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v1">

  <assemblyIdentity version="1.0.0.0" name="KidShell.SecurityHost" />

  <!--
    asInvoker, with the elevation requirement enforced in code instead.

    THE COMMENT THAT USED TO BE HERE WAS WRONG.

    It said KidShell launched this binary with the "runas" verb, so the
    manifest did not need to ask. KidShell did not. The production client
    started it with UseShellExecute off and the standard streams redirected,
    which ignores a verb and cannot carry one. The security property the
    comment claimed therefore did not exist, and the helper refused every
    request on every child account. See BrokerTransportDefectTests.

    The manifest still says asInvoker, now for reasons that are true of this
    build:

      * its normal mode is a Windows SERVICE. The service control manager
        starts it as LocalSystem, and requireAdministrator means nothing to
        the SCM;

      * the self-test mode must run unelevated on a build agent, or the check
        that proves the broker rejects hostile input would be the one check
        nobody could run;

      * the policy approval mode is launched WITH the runas verb, by
        ElevatedCommitApprovalChannel, which raises the prompt regardless of
        what the manifest says, and redirects nothing, so the verb is
        honoured. That is the one place in this product where a consent
        prompt belongs.

    Every mode that needs privilege checks for it at runtime and exits if it
    does not have it.

    (Two hyphens in a row are not legal inside an XML comment. A manifest
    that contains a pair does not parse, the binary will not start at all,
    and Windows reports it as a side-by-side configuration error that says
    nothing about manifests. The option names are therefore spelled out in
    prose above rather than written with their flags.)
  -->
  <trustInfo xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v2">
    <security>
      <requestedPrivileges xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
        <requestedExecutionLevel level="asInvoker" uiAccess="false" />
      </requestedPrivileges>
    </security>
  </trustInfo>

  <compatibility xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:compatibility.v1">
    <application>
      <!-- Windows 10 and 11. -->
      <supportedOS Id="{8e0f7a12-bfb3-4fe8-b9a5-48fd50a15a9a}" />
    </application>
  </compatibility>

</assembly>
